Import "../Physics.lingua"

Begin with systems, change and physical explanations (@physics-core: 0, is #chapter, #hephaestus-core "Choose-a-physical-system-and-a-question.lingua#r_03633FE49B5013971613976F7D") { r_0C0B264F8BB7FF26E566E6B5DB
Physics seeks explanations of matter, motion and interactions that can be tested against observations. Start by identifying a system: the object or region about which a claim is made. Its surroundings matter through interactions across the chosen boundary. A model keeps features relevant to a question and states which features it leaves out.

A cart changes position from 1 m to 7 m in 3 s along a chosen axis. Its displacement is 6 m and its average velocity is 2 m/s. Neither number establishes the velocity at every moment. If it moves forward and then returns, distance travelled can exceed displacement. Position needs a reference and direction; elapsed time is a difference between two time readings.

For an ideal 2 kg cart with a constant net force of 4 N along that axis, Newton's relation F = ma predicts an acceleration of 2 m/s². Acceleration describes the rate of change of velocity. Starting from rest, this model predicts 6 m/s after 3 s and a displacement of 9 m. These are predictions for the specified constant-force model, not deductions from the earlier two position readings. Friction, changing force and measurement uncertainty can change the comparison with an actual cart.

Conservation laws help connect systems. Energy can cross a boundary by work or heat; accounting for its amount does not by itself determine how rapidly a process occurs or what useful conversion is possible. Momentum, charge and material balances ask related but different questions. Later thermal, electrical, quantum and relativistic theories introduce additional concepts and limits; introductory mechanics is one branch of physics, not its complete explanation.

**Shared preparation:** use [Units and dimensions](../../inquiry/quantitative-literacy/foundations/Units-and-dimensions.lingua#r_3A511KGNX59K2YHKT83NM38NE1) before combining quantities, and [Specify the measurand](../../inquiry/metrology/foundations/Question-and-measurand.lingua#r_7ZNCJFEGXNJSGJJJKGYH6MGFE6) before comparing a prediction with an observation. The existing [thermal formation](../thermodynamics-and-heat-transfer/Thermodynamics-and-heat-transfer.lingua#r_6KQBZNJM6WNV9A69R3DZY1NH7E) and [electromagnetic formation](../electromagnetism/Electromagnetism.lingua#r_5TJPESCTTNAKY66JH2QNQYN1PY) contain deeper readings and investigations to return to when needed.

**Further shared explanation:** [Work, kinetic energy, potential and mechanical power](../../engineering/mechanical-engineering-and-machine-design/dynamics/Work-kinetic-energy-potential-and-mechanical-power.lingua#r_4VJ79D0Q8KT7CC39Q1GRSDAAZ8) provides the derivation and primary readings to return to after the necessary vector and calculus preparation. The constant-force example above needs only arithmetic.

**Work:** draw a position-time graph consistent with the first cart example and another with the same endpoints but different motion. For the constant-force example, compute the final kinetic energy, mv²/2, and compare it with work F times displacement. Both give 36 J. State the assumptions that make this equality applicable. Explain how a force measurement and a motion measurement could test different parts of the account without treating repeated agreement as proof of universal validity.

**Review:** distinguish observation, definition, model, prediction and conservation. Keep uncertainty and the model's domain visible. The area will develop mechanics, matter and thermal behavior, fields and waves, quantum/statistical reasoning, relativity and cosmic systems before specialist depth is extended.

**Return:** [Physics — matter, motion, fields and physical evidence](../Physics.lingua#r_04A5CP8RTBERZEN4XCCDQ3T47W).

**Continue in the physics core:** [Choose a physical system and a question](Choose-a-physical-system-and-a-question.lingua#r_03633FE49B5013971613976F7D).

